The major difference between software evaluation and LLM evaluation is what they focus on. Software evaluation checks whether an application works correctly according to its requirements, such as whether features work, the system is reliable, secure, fast, and free from errors. It usually has a predictable expected output, so testing can be done using unit testing, integration testing, system testing, and performance testing. On the other hand, LLM evaluation focuses on the quality of the AI-generated response. It checks whether the answer is correct, relevant, useful, complete, safe, and free from hallucinations. Unlike traditional software, an LLM can produce different answers for the same question, and multiple answers may be correct. Therefore, LLM evaluation often requires metrics, test datasets, human evaluation, or an LLM-as-a-judge approach. In simple terms, software evaluation asks "Does the software work correctly?", while LLM evaluation asks "Does the AI provide a correct, relevant, useful, and trustworthy answer?" 


1. What are LLM Evaluations?

LLM evaluation means systematically checking how well a Large Language Model performs. When we use an LLM such as GPT, Claude, Gemini, or Llama, we cannot simply assume that the model is good because it can generate fluent answers. We need to test whether its answers are correct, relevant, useful, safe, consistent, and able to follow instructions. For example, if we ask an LLM a mathematical question, we want to know whether it gives the correct answer. If we use it for coding, we want to know whether the generated code works correctly. If we use it in a RAG application, we want to know whether the answer is actually supported by the retrieved documents. Therefore, LLM evaluation is the process of testing and measuring the capabilities and quality of an LLM so that we can understand its strengths, weaknesses, and suitability for a particular use case.

2. What is the difference between LLM Evals and Metrics?

LLM evaluation and metrics are related, but they are not exactly the same thing. An evaluation is the overall process of checking the performance of an LLM or AI application, while a metric is a specific measurement used to determine that performance. For example, suppose we want to evaluate a RAG chatbot. We may want to check whether the answer is correct, whether it is relevant to the user's question, and whether it is supported by the retrieved documents. These are evaluation areas, and we can use metrics such as accuracy, relevance, faithfulness, precision, recall, or a score generated by an evaluator model to measure them. In simple terms, evaluation tells us what we want to check, while a metric tells us how we measure it. For example, "Is the answer supported by the document?" is an evaluation question, while "faithfulness score = 0.92" is a metric result.

3. What are the different types of LLM Evaluations?

LLM evaluations can broadly be divided into Model Evaluation and Application Evaluation. Model evaluation focuses on the underlying LLM itself and tries to understand its general capabilities, such as reasoning, coding, mathematics, language understanding, knowledge, and instruction following. Application evaluation focuses on the complete AI application that we have built using the LLM. For example, if we build a RAG chatbot, we don't only evaluate the LLM. We also evaluate the document retrieval, prompt, retrieved context, final answer, hallucination, latency, and overall user experience. Therefore, model evaluation tells us how capable the model is, whereas application evaluation tells us how well our complete AI system performs for a particular business or user requirement.

4. What is Model Evaluation?

Model evaluation means evaluating the capabilities of the LLM itself. Here, we are interested in understanding how well the underlying model performs on different types of tasks. For example, we may test whether a model can solve mathematical problems, write and understand code, answer general knowledge questions, summarize documents, reason through complex problems, follow instructions, or understand different languages. Model evaluation is usually performed using a predefined dataset or benchmark containing many test questions and expected evaluation criteria. Multiple models can then be tested using the same benchmark and their results can be compared. For example, if Model A scores 85% and Model B scores 92% on a particular benchmark, Model B performed better on that benchmark. However, this does not necessarily mean Model B is always better, because the best model depends on the particular task and application we are building.

5. What are Model Benchmarks?

A model benchmark is a standardized test or dataset used to measure and compare the capabilities of different AI models. The idea is similar to giving the same examination to different students and comparing their scores. A benchmark may contain questions or tasks related to reasoning, mathematics, coding, language understanding, knowledge, or other capabilities. For example, suppose three models are tested on the same reasoning benchmark. Model A gets 80%, Model B gets 88%, and Model C gets 91%. Based on that benchmark, Model C performed the best. AI engineers use benchmarks to compare models, understand model capabilities, select candidate models, and track improvements when new model versions are released. However, benchmarks should not be treated as the only source of truth because a model that performs very well on a general benchmark may not necessarily perform best on your specific application.

6. How do AI Engineers use Model Benchmarks?

AI engineers use model benchmarks mainly to compare models and understand which model may be suitable for a particular task. Suppose an engineer is building a coding assistant and has three different LLMs available. Instead of selecting a model randomly, they can compare the models on coding-related benchmarks and also test them on their own coding examples. Similarly, if they are building a mathematical reasoning application, they can compare models on mathematics and reasoning benchmarks. Benchmarks provide a common way to compare models under similar conditions. However, experienced AI engineers do not rely only on public benchmarks. They also create custom evaluation datasets that represent their actual business use case. This is important because benchmark performance is general, while application-specific evaluation tells us how the model performs on the exact problems users will encounter.

7. What is Application Evaluation?

Application evaluation means evaluating the complete AI application rather than only evaluating the underlying LLM. An AI application usually contains multiple components, such as prompts, an LLM, databases, retrieval systems, tools, APIs, business logic, and sometimes memory. All of these components can affect the final result. For example, consider a RAG chatbot. The user asks a question, the retriever searches the knowledge base, relevant documents are retrieved, those documents are added to a prompt, the LLM generates an answer, and the answer is returned to the user. Even if the LLM itself is excellent, the application can still give a bad answer if the retriever retrieves the wrong documents or the prompt is poorly designed. Therefore, application evaluation checks the end-to-end performance of the entire AI system.

8. What is the difference between a Model and an AI Application?

A model is one component of an AI application, while an AI application is the complete system built around that model. For example, an LLM can be thought of as the brain that understands a question and generates a response, but a real AI application may also need a database, retrieval system, prompts, APIs, tools, authentication, business logic, and a user interface. A useful analogy is a smartphone. The processor inside the smartphone is an important component, but evaluating the processor is not the same as evaluating the entire smartphone. The processor may be very powerful, but the smartphone could still have poor battery life, a bad camera, or a poor user interface. Similarly, an LLM can be very powerful, but the complete AI application can still perform poorly because other components are not working correctly.

9. What should we evaluate in an AI Application?

When evaluating an AI application, we should evaluate more than just whether the LLM generates an answer. First, we should check the correctness of the answer, meaning whether the information provided is actually correct. We should check relevance, meaning whether the response directly answers the user's question. We should check faithfulness, especially in RAG applications, to determine whether the answer is supported by the retrieved context rather than being invented by the model. We should also evaluate hallucination, because an LLM may confidently generate information that is not true. In addition, we can evaluate instruction following, safety, consistency, latency, token usage, cost, and overall user satisfaction. For a RAG system specifically, we should also evaluate the quality of the retrieval process because even a good LLM cannot produce a reliable answer if it receives incorrect or irrelevant context.

10. What is Faithfulness in LLM Evaluation?

Faithfulness measures whether the generated answer is supported by the information provided to the model. This is particularly important in RAG applications. Suppose a company's document says, "Employees can work from home two days per week." A user asks, "How many days can employees work from home?" If the model answers "Employees can work from home two days per week," the answer is faithful because it is supported by the document. But if the model answers "Employees can work from home five days per week," the answer is not faithful because that information was not supported by the provided context. Therefore, faithfulness helps us determine whether the LLM is using the available information correctly rather than inventing information.

11. What is Relevance in LLM Evaluation?

Relevance measures whether the response is actually related to the user's question and provides useful information. An answer can be factually correct but still be irrelevant. For example, if a user asks, "What is LangGraph?" and the model gives a long explanation about Python programming without explaining LangGraph, the response may contain correct information but it does not properly answer the question. A relevant response should focus on the user's question and provide the information needed to answer it. In RAG systems, relevance can also be used to evaluate whether the retrieved documents or chunks are relevant to the user's query.

12. What is Hallucination in LLM Evaluation?

Hallucination occurs when an LLM generates information that is false, unsupported, or made up but presents it as if it were correct. This is one of the major concerns when evaluating LLM applications. For example, if we ask a model about a company and it invents an employee's name, job title, or company policy that does not exist, the model has hallucinated. Hallucination is especially dangerous in applications such as healthcare, finance, legal systems, customer support, and enterprise RAG systems because users may rely on the generated information. Therefore, application evaluation should include tests designed to identify and reduce hallucinations.

13. What is Retrieval Evaluation in a RAG Application?

In a RAG application, retrieval evaluation checks whether the system retrieves the right information before sending it to the LLM. A typical RAG system works like this: the user asks a question, the retriever searches the knowledge base, relevant documents are retrieved, and those documents are given to the LLM to generate the final answer. If the retriever returns irrelevant documents, the LLM may not have enough correct information to answer the question. Therefore, we can evaluate metrics such as precision, recall, context relevance, and retrieval accuracy to understand the quality of the retrieval process. This is an important part of application evaluation because the final answer depends heavily on the quality of the retrieved context.

14. What is the main difference between Model Evaluation and Application Evaluation?

The main difference is that Model Evaluation evaluates the LLM itself, whereas Application Evaluation evaluates the complete AI system built using the LLM. Model evaluation asks questions such as, "How good is this model at reasoning, coding, mathematics, or instruction following?" Application evaluation asks questions such as, "Does my RAG chatbot provide correct answers? Does it retrieve the right documents? Does it hallucinate? Is it fast enough? Is it useful to the user?" Model evaluation is therefore more general and is useful for comparing models, while application evaluation is more specific to the system that you have built.

15. Why can a good LLM still produce a bad AI application?

A good LLM does not automatically guarantee a good AI application because the final performance depends on all the components in the system. For example, suppose you use a highly capable LLM in a RAG chatbot. If your documents are poorly processed, your chunking is bad, your embeddings are poor, your retriever returns irrelevant documents, or your prompt is badly designed, the final answer may still be incorrect. Similarly, if an application uses an external API and that API fails, the application may not work even though the LLM itself is excellent. Therefore, we need application-level evaluation to identify problems outside the model itself.

16. What is the easiest way to remember Model Evaluation vs Application Evaluation?

The easiest way to remember it is through the smartphone analogy. Think of the LLM as the processor inside a smartphone. You can test the processor to determine how powerful it is, which is similar to Model Evaluation. But if you want to know whether the entire smartphone is good, you need to evaluate the battery, camera, display, applications, performance, usability, and other components. This is similar to Application Evaluation. In the same way, an LLM may have excellent benchmark scores, but your AI application may still perform poorly because of problems in retrieval, prompts, tools, data, APIs, or business logic.

17. Final Summary

In simple terms, LLM evaluation is the process of measuring how well an LLM or an AI application performs. There are two important levels: Model Evaluation and Application Evaluation. Model evaluation focuses on the capabilities of the underlying LLM, such as reasoning, coding, mathematics, knowledge, and instruction following. Benchmarks are commonly used to compare different models. Application evaluation, on the other hand, evaluates the complete AI system, including the LLM, prompts, retrieval system, database, tools, APIs, and business logic. In an AI or RAG application, we commonly evaluate correctness, relevance, faithfulness, hallucination, retrieval quality, instruction following, safety, latency, cost, and overall usefulness. The most important thing to remember is: Model Evaluation asks "How good is the LLM?" whereas Application Evaluation asks "How good is the complete AI application for its intended purpose?"
